# Assignment 1.2 — Development Environment Setup

## Solution

First, I installed Visual Studio Code from the official website, https://code.visualstudio.com/. Next, I downloaded and installed the LTS version of Node.js from https://nodejs.org/. After installing the software, I opened Visual Studio Code and installed the Live Server and Prettier extensions from the Extensions Marketplace.

Then, I created a folder named `web-development-practice` on my computer and opened it in Visual Studio Code. Inside the folder, I created three files: `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.js`.

I added HTML code to `index.html` to create the structure of a webpage, CSS code to `style.css` to style the webpage, and JavaScript code to `script.js` to add interactivity.

After saving all the files, I opened the VS Code terminal by selecting Terminal → New Terminal and ran the following commands to verify the installation of Node.js and npm.

```bash
node -v
npm -v
```

Both commands displayed version numbers, confirming that Node.js and npm were installed successfully.

Finally, I opened `index.html` in Visual Studio Code, right-clicked inside the editor, and selected Open with Live Server. The webpage opened in the browser, displaying the heading, paragraphs, and button. I clicked the button to verify that the JavaScript code was working correctly.

## Result

The web development environment was successfully set up. Visual Studio Code, Node.js, Live Server, and Prettier were installed. The project folder and all three files were created, the Node.js and npm versions were verified, and the HTML page was successfully opened in the browser using Live Server.

## Conclusion

This assignment helped me understand how to set up a basic web development environment and how HTML, CSS, and JavaScript work together to create an interactive webpage.